@@ category: 1D DP

@@@ problem 70 climbing-stairs
title: Climbing Stairs
difficulty: Easy
topics: Math, Dynamic Programming, Memoization
params: n
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
You are climbing a staircase with `n` steps. Each time you can climb **1 or 2** steps. In how many distinct ways can you reach the top?

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 45`

=== hints
- To stand on step `i`, your last move came from step `i-1` or step `i-2`.
- `ways(i) = ways(i-1) + ways(i-2)`: Fibonacci. You only need the last two values.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int climbStairs(int n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::climbStairs);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) { return lc::lines(g.irange(1, 45)); });
}

=== approach
Let `ways(i)` be the number of ways to reach step `i`. The last move onto step `i` is either a 1-step from `i-1` or a 2-step from `i-2`, and these cases don't overlap, so `ways(i) = ways(i-1) + ways(i-2)` with `ways(0) = ways(1) = 1`. That's the Fibonacci recurrence. Keep only the two previous values: O(n) time, O(1) space.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int climbStairs(int n) {
        int prev2 = 1, prev1 = 1;          // ways(i-2), ways(i-1)
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
            int cur = prev1 + prev2;       // last move was 1 step or 2 steps
            prev2 = prev1;
            prev1 = cur;
        }
        return prev1;
    }
};

=== examples
2
> 2
: 1+1 or 2.

3
> 3
: 1+1+1, 1+2, 2+1.

=== tests
1
> 1

5
> 8

45
> 1836311903

@@@ problem 198 house-robber
title: House Robber
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Dynamic Programming
params: nums
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1

=== description
Houses along a street hold `nums[i]` money each. You can't rob two **adjacent** houses (the alarm would go off). Return the maximum amount you can rob.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 100`
- `0 <= nums[i] <= 400`

=== hints
- For each house, either rob it (then skip the previous one) or skip it.
- `best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + nums[i])`.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int rob(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::rob);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.ints(std::max(n, 1), 0, 400)); });
}

=== approach
Let `best(i)` be the most money from houses `0..i`. For house `i`: skip it and keep `best(i-1)`, or rob it and add its money to `best(i-2)` (house `i-1` must then be skipped). Take the larger. Only the previous two values are needed.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int rob(vector<int>& nums) {
        int skip = 0, take = 0;            // best(i-2), best(i-1)
        for (int x : nums) {
            int cur = max(take, skip + x); // skip house i, or rob it
            skip = take;
            take = cur;
        }
        return take;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,2,3,1]
> 4
: Rob houses 0 and 2: 1 + 3 = 4.

[2,7,9,3,1]
> 12
: Rob houses 0, 2 and 4: 2 + 9 + 1 = 12.

=== tests
[0]
> 0

[5,1]
> 5

[2,1,1,2]
> 4

[400,400,400]
> 800

@@@ problem 139 word-break
title: Word Break
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Hash Table, String, Dynamic Programming, Trie, Memoization
params: s, wordDict
time: O(n · L²)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..262144 time=n space=n unit=s.length_(words_≤_5_letters)

=== description
Given a string `s` and a dictionary `wordDict`, return `true` if `s` can be split into a sequence of one or more dictionary words (separated by nothing). Words may be reused any number of times.

=== constraints
- `1 <= s.length <= 300`
- `1 <= wordDict.length <= 1000`, `1 <= wordDict[i].length <= 20`
- all strings are lowercase; dictionary words are unique

=== hints
- `ok[i]` = can the prefix `s[0..i)` be segmented? `ok[0] = true`.
- `ok[i]` is true if some word ends at `i` and `ok[i - len(word)]` is true. Only word lengths that exist in the dictionary matter.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool wordBreak(string s, vector<string>& wordDict) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::wordBreak);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        if (n > 40)   // benchmark: the classic worst case, answer false after full DP
            return lc::lines(std::string(n - 1, 'a') + "b", std::vector<std::string>{"a", "aa", "aaa", "aaaa", "aaaaa"});
        std::set<std::string> dict;
        for (int i = 0, k = g.irange(1, 5); i < k; ++i) dict.insert(g.lower(g.irange(1, 3), 2));
        std::vector<std::string> d(dict.begin(), dict.end());
        std::string s;
        while ((int)s.size() < std::max(1, n)) s += g.pick(d);
        if (g.coin(0.4)) s[g.irange(0, (int)s.size() - 1)] = 'a' + g.irange(0, 2);
        return lc::lines(s, d);
    });
}

=== approach
Dynamic programming over prefixes: `ok[i]` says whether `s[0..i)` splits into words. `ok[0] = true` (empty prefix). For each end `i`, try every possible word length `len` (only lengths present in the dictionary, at most the longest word): if `ok[i - len]` holds and `s.substr(i - len, len)` is a word, then `ok[i] = true`.

With `n = |s|` and longest word `L`, there are O(n · L) substring checks, each hashing O(L) characters.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool wordBreak(string s, vector<string>& wordDict) {
        unordered_set<string> words(wordDict.begin(), wordDict.end());
        size_t maxLen = 0;
        for (auto &w : wordDict) maxLen = max(maxLen, w.size());
        int n = s.size();
        vector<char> ok(n + 1, false);
        ok[0] = true;                                     // empty prefix
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
            for (int len = 1; len <= (int)maxLen && len <= i; ++len) {
                // last word is s[i-len, i)
                if (ok[i - len] && words.count(s.substr(i - len, len))) {
                    ok[i] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        return ok[n];
    }
};

=== examples
"leetcode"
["leet","code"]
> true

"applepenapple"
["apple","pen"]
> true
: Words may be reused.

"catsandog"
["cats","dog","sand","and","cat"]
> false

=== tests
"a"
["b"]
> false

"aaaaaaa"
["aaaa","aaa"]
> true

"cars"
["car","ca","rs"]
> true

"aaab"
["a","aa"]
> false

@@@ problem 322 coin-change
title: Coin Change
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Dynamic Programming, Breadth-First Search
params: coins, amount
time: O(amount · coins)
space: O(amount)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=amount_(5_coins)

=== description
Given coin denominations `coins` and a total `amount`, return the **fewest** coins needed to make up exactly that amount, or `-1` if it's impossible. You have an unlimited supply of each coin.

=== constraints
- `1 <= coins.length <= 12`
- `1 <= coins[i] <= 2^31 - 1`
- `0 <= amount <= 10^4`

=== hints
- Greedy (always take the biggest coin) fails, e.g. coins `[1,3,4]`, amount 6.
- `best[a] = 1 + min(best[a - c])` over coins `c <= a`, with `best[0] = 0`.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int coinChange(vector<int>& coins, int amount) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::coinChange);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        if (n > 40) return lc::lines(std::vector<int>{7, 13, 29, 101, 997}, n);
        return lc::lines(g.distinct(g.irange(1, 4), 1, 12), g.irange(0, 4 * n));
    });
}

=== approach
Bottom-up DP: `best[a]` is the fewest coins summing to `a`, with `best[0] = 0` and "infinity" meaning impossible. For every amount `a` from 1 to `amount`, try each coin `c <= a` as the **last** coin: `best[a] = min(best[a], best[a - c] + 1)`. The answer is `best[amount]`, or `-1` if it is still infinity. The work is O(amount · number of coins).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int coinChange(vector<int>& coins, int amount) {
        const int INF = INT_MAX / 2;
        vector<int> best(amount + 1, INF);
        best[0] = 0;                                   // zero coins make zero
        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; ++a)
            for (int c : coins)
                if (c <= a && best[a - c] + 1 < best[a])
                    best[a] = best[a - c] + 1;         // use coin c last
        return best[amount] >= INF ? -1 : best[amount];
    }
};

=== examples
[1,2,5]
11
> 3
: 11 = 5 + 5 + 1.

[2]
3
> -1

[1]
0
> 0

=== tests
[1,3,4]
6
> 2

[2147483647]
2
> -1

[186,419,83,408]
6249
> 20

@@@ problem 300 longest-increasing-subsequence
title: Longest Increasing Subsequence
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search, Dynamic Programming
params: nums
time: O(n log n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=nlogn space=n

=== description
Given an integer array `nums`, return the length of the longest **strictly increasing subsequence**.

Follow-up: an O(n²) DP is straightforward; can you reach O(n log n)?

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 2500`
- `-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4`

=== hints
- O(n²): `lis[i] = 1 + max(lis[j])` over `j < i` with `nums[j] < nums[i]`.
- O(n log n): keep `tails[k]`, the smallest possible tail of an increasing subsequence of length `k + 1`. It stays sorted, so binary search where each number goes.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLIS(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::lengthOfLIS);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.ints(std::max(n, 1), -10000 - n, 10000 + n)); });
}

=== approach
**Patience sorting.** Maintain `tails`, where `tails[k]` is the smallest tail value of any increasing subsequence of length `k + 1` seen so far. This array is always strictly increasing.

For each number `x`, find the first tail `>= x` (binary search, `lower_bound`). If there is none, `x` extends the longest subsequence: append it. Otherwise replace that tail with `x`: a subsequence of that length can now end with a smaller value, which is never worse. The length of `tails` is the answer. Each step costs O(log n).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLIS(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> tails;   // tails[k] = smallest tail of an increasing subsequence of length k+1
        for (int x : nums) {
            auto it = lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);   // first tail >= x
            if (it == tails.end()) tails.push_back(x);              // extends the longest one
            else *it = x;                                           // better (smaller) tail
        }
        return tails.size();
    }
};

=== examples
[10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18]
> 4
: For example [2,3,7,101].

[0,1,0,3,2,3]
> 4

[7,7,7,7,7,7,7]
> 1
: Strictly increasing, so equal values don't count.

=== tests
[1]
> 1

[5,4,3,2,1]
> 1

[1,3,6,7,9,4,10,5,6]
> 6

[4,10,4,3,8,9]
> 3
